    "review_depth": "Read the main 2023 Cell article and its full STAR Methods, including the recording, preprocessing, model evaluation and results sections; checked the relevant figure legends and selected supporting captions. Read the 2024 Opinion's historical framing, mechanism discussion, receiver-evidence conclusion and open questions. Also checked the exact Dryad record, Dryad's live CC0 policy and the linked Zenodo v1 classifier record. The Cell publisher endpoint was inaccessible, so its linked full-paper copy was used. No audio, archive, code or model was downloaded or run; no result was independently reproduced.",
    "finding": "Khait et al. report airborne ultrasonic emissions from stressed tomato and tobacco in an acoustic box and greenhouse, and classify plant condition from recordings. The system used paired CM16 ultrasound microphones per plant at 500 kHz, with a 20 kHz high-pass filter; an event counted as plant-associated only when both microphones aimed at that plant recorded it. They report leave-one-plant-out evaluation: a 1-hour greenhouse dry-versus-irrigated classification reached 84% balanced accuracy, and a two-range soil-moisture classification in a nine-day dry-down of 23 tomato plants reached 81%. These are emission and physiological-state results, not decoded messages or tested receiver responses.",
    "limitations": "The experiments used specialized ultrasonic hardware and controlled acoustic-box/greenhouse settings; ordinary phone microphones are not an equivalent sensor. The plant-held-out folds reduce leakage from repeated sounds of the same plant, but do not establish generalization to new sites, devices, species or field noise. The paper's 3–5 m animal detection range is estimated from reported sound levels and published hearing thresholds, not measured animal response. Nardini et al.'s 2024 Opinion places acoustic emissions in a long history of plant physiology and discusses xylem cavitation and other physical mechanisms; it says ecological receiver use still needed evidence at that time. The later moth study in source 16 supplies a bounded receiver-response result in one moth/plant setting, not evidence of plant intention or a general language. No independent reproduction or qualified plant-acoustics review has been obtained. Dryad lists the recordings under its CC0 repository policy, but the archive contents were not inspected; the Zenodo classifier record has no license stated.",
