    "review_depth": "On October 28, rechecked the official eLife version of record and its public peer-review/author-response page, focusing on Table 1, Methods and Data Availability. Compared those sections with the existing aggregate-only report for the exact Mendeley Data v1 data.xlsx file. The article says all tables and code are available from Mendeley. Its Methods mention a lower-rate 2-click/min oviposition repeat; Table 1 reports no outcome row for that condition. The only bat-playback passage describes a separate flight-response check of hearing after deafening. It does not map the workbook's Bat egg-cluster label to that check. The public Mendeley Data v1 inventory lists three folders with 32 files (24 CSV, four R, two MATLAB and two XLSX); on October 24, one 51,160-byte data.xlsx file was acquired to ignored external storage and matched the file-panel SHA-256. A standard-library aggregate-only audit matched all five main Table 1 repetition, paired-observation and egg-cluster totals. No workbook formulas or deposited code were executed, no inferential model was fit, the other files and publisher source-data ZIP remain uninspected, and no independent specialist review was obtained.",
    "finding": "In controlled choices, female Egyptian cotton leafworm moths (Spodoptera littoralis) changed where they laid egg clusters when playback of drought-stressed tomato-plant clicks was present. The preference depended on the other cues: playback attracted oviposition in a plant-free arena, while moths preferred the silent side when healthy tomato plants were offered on both sides. Deafening removed the plant-free playback preference; male-moth click controls did not produce a detected preference. A separate long-arena experiment used a lower 30-click/min rate and found egg clusters concentrated near the playback and feeder rather than the silent edge. A checksum-matched aggregate audit of the single Mendeley data.xlsx file also matched the five main Table 1 tuples for repetitions, paired arena/date observations and egg-cluster totals; this is a source-table consistency check, not a statistical-model reproduction.",
    "limitations": "This is one moth species, one plant species and a controlled laboratory/greenhouse study, not evidence that plants intentionally communicate or that animals can be translated by AI. The authors characterize drought sounds as a likely cue rather than an evolved message. The main choice experiments used 60 clicks/min; the authors measured at most 20 clicks/min from 45 drying seedlings in a 50-hour recording and argue that a dense patch could raise exposure. The later 30-click/min gradient setup differs in arena and subject design, so it is not a direct replication of the main choice trials. Preferences also reverse with plant context; other visual, olfactory and tactile cues complicate interpretation. The Mendeley inventory contains tabular and XY-point files, not listed audio recordings; it cannot serve as an animal-audio benchmark. Although one workbook was checksum-verified and parsed, this does not verify the remaining files, cross-file joins, dependencies or correspondence to the publisher ZIP. Reviewer comments raised ecological realism, effect-size presentation, availability of raw data/code and species generalization; Talk2Nature has not independently reproduced or reanalysed the results. The workbook contains a lower-rate 2-click/min condition and a Bat label outside the reported Table 1 oviposition outcomes. The Methods mention the 2-click/min repeat, but Table 1 has no outcome row for it. The only bat playback in the version of record is a separate flight-response check of hearing after deafening. The article does not map the workbook's Bat egg-cluster label to that check; keep the row excluded. The full eLife source-data ZIP and the rest of the Mendeley files remain unacquired.",
