    "finding": "BirdCODE is a large-scale bird-vocalization event detector and species classifier, not a decoder of intended meaning. The paper reports framewise per-species scores at 7.6 Hz, postprocessed into event boundaries, and applies them to 1.3 million citizen-science recordings. Its four demonstrations examine vocalization duration across phylogeny, seasonal features, geographic variation, and cross-species temporal associations. For the latter, the authors ask whether species B vocalizes within five seconds after species A and compare with circularly shifted timing nulls; this is a useful hypothesis-generating association, not experimental proof of a reply or receiver comprehension. The official software already provides a human-usable research pipeline: local selection tables, evaluation configs, sharded inference with lineage files and public precomputed detections. Its README defaults include 32 kHz, a 0.5 threshold, merging same-label events with gaps under one second and NMS at IoU 0.8.",
