    "finding": "The authors studied six context categories of adult-female elephant rumbles, including non-overlapping calls and overlapping multi-caller choruses. They report above-baseline context prediction for single and chorused signals and cross-family-category performance, with weaker performance on held-out callers/families than within-family splits. In the separate convergence analysis, 92 exchanges from the heavily sampled EB family met the multi-caller criteria; acoustic convergence was reported for Cadenced, Contact and Greeting contexts, but not the other three. The study is useful evidence that overlapping vocal events can retain context-associated acoustic patterns and that social coordination can be studied without treating every recording as one isolated call. These are observational context associations, not a decoded message or demonstrated receiver comprehension.",
    "limitations": "Context labels were assigned in real time by experienced observers using behavior and social surroundings; a second observer was consulted when uncertain but did not independently annotate all calls, so formal inter-rater reliability was unavailable. Only cases deemed certain were retained, yet the authors acknowledge remaining contextual heterogeneity and label noise. Held-out-caller evaluation was available for EB single rumbles only; chorused context models did not hold callers out because samples contain multiple caller IDs and were limited. Cross-family results combine one repeatedly sampled EB family and a pooled non-EB family category, with lower accuracy. The convergence subset contains 92 exchanges from EB and relies partly on inferred caller IDs, though the authors report a sensitivity analysis for ambiguous identities. No playback test measured receiver responses. The paper describes 200 iterations per convergence permutation test; the pinned code computes empirical tail fractions from those iterations without a plus-one correction, while the article reports a value below .001 for one test. This leaves the exact p-value resolution/estimator to clarify before reusing that statistic; Talk2Nature did not rerun or invalidate the analysis. The analysis repository has no visible license, and data terms are unresolved.",
    "lesson": "Represent a chorus or overlapping interval as an event with multiple possible callers, explicit overlap and uncertainty, not as forced single-speaker truth. Keep observer-assigned context and its confidence/provenance separate from acoustic predictions. When grouping tests, hold out the relevant caller, session and family/site where the design supports it; distinguish within-family prediction from transfer. Require receiver behavior or playback evidence before a comprehension claim. This paper does not change Talk2Nature's selected first computational question or admit the elephant corpus.",
