    "finding": "BioDCASE 2025 Task 3 is a concrete example of designing bird-vocalization detection around a constrained recorder: Yellowhammer presence is scored on an ESP32-S3-Korvo-2, with average precision reported beside model size, RAM and processing time. The development record describes 209 source songs from 10 individuals, assembled into a playback track and re-recorded across distances and forest/grassland settings; a source song can recur up to 15 times. Its stated split reserves different individuals for training, validation and evaluation. One selected Toronto Metropolitan University report compares a shallow CNN with compact GFCC and spectral-flux features. It illustrates an edge tradeoff: small classifiers can move much of the compute and memory cost into feature extraction, so inference-only timing is incomplete. The official results page and participant report describe different metrics and the exact mapping between report methods and submitted scoreboard entry was not established. These challenge results are not a common cross-dataset comparison or evidence of Talk2Nature performance. The development set is CC BY 4.0, while its 224.7 MB archive exceeds our 5 MiB collector limit. The GitHub baseline declares Apache-2.0. The evaluation v2 record now lists both audio and binary labels and shows no license.",
    "limitations": "This is a single-species, short-clip detection challenge on a named embedded board, not a ten-second general bird-presence benchmark, phone test, behavior/context model, receiver-response study or communication result. The replay design repeats source songs across distances/settings; clip-level random splitting would leak song identity and inflate apparent independence. The public development description lists training positives at 16 kHz and 2 seconds, validation positives at 48 kHz and 0.5–3 seconds, and validation negatives at 16 kHz and 2 seconds. These reported differences raise a possible sample-rate/duration shortcut or validation distribution-shift concern; no audio or code was inspected, so actual leakage or its effect is unestablished. In the selected participant report, the abstract and results table place the three methods between 89.10% and 92.65% test accuracy, but the Method B results prose states 90.18% versus 91.18% in the table, and the Method C prose states 94.01% versus 89.10% in the table. The report also describes 14 kHz preprocessing and a Method C 5–8 kHz feature band, where the upper portion would be above the 7 kHz Nyquist limit if that stated rate applies; the exact implementation was not verified. The organizer table reports average precision, a different metric, and its model-to-report mapping was not resolved. Do not treat these discrepancies as a reproduced benchmark result. Evaluation labels are public and rights are not stated in the Zenodo record, so this is not a concealed holdout or a cleared new benchmark. Other reports, exact source version and phone deployment were not tested.",
    "lesson": "Apply the useful engineering practice to future Talk2Nature detector studies: report task-specific event performance together with device, preprocessing, model size, latency and peak memory; include feature-extraction cost rather than only model-forward time; preserve original recording and sample-rate/clip-duration provenance; split by independent source song, caller and acquisition group; and audit label balance across device, rate, duration, distance and environment before trusting a score. Treat conflicting report metrics and unclear signal-rate processing as questions to reproduce, not results to borrow. Keep the ten-second passive bird-presence question distinct from this Yellowhammer task. An on-device, user-started detector may later help a phone work locally with low latency and less audio transfer, but this challenge alone does not select a phone model or justify always-on capture or central audio storage. No model or data should enter AutoResearch from this metadata review.",
